        titleTr: "Aslan ile Fare",
        wordCount: 510,
        readTime: "4 dk",
        text: `A great Lion was sleeping peacefully under a big tree in the hot savannah. His golden mane shone in the sunlight, and his heavy paws rested on the cool sand. Near his feet, a tiny field Mouse was searching for small seeds and berries. The little Mouse did not notice the sleeping giant. By mistake, she ran right across the Lion's big black nose.\n\nThe Lion woke up with a deep, angry roar. He placed his massive paw firmly on the tiny Mouse. He opened his enormous jaws to swallow her in one bite. "Please, King of the Jungle!" squeaked the terrified little Mouse. "Forgive me this time! I did not mean to wake you up. If you spare my humble life, I will always remember your great kindness. Perhaps one day, I may even be able to help you in return!"\n\nThe Lion laughed loudly at the idea. The sound of his laughter shook the trees around them. "A tiny little mouse helping the King of Beasts? That is the funniest thing I have ever heard in my life!" the Lion laughed. But because he was in a generous mood, he lifted his heavy paw and let the grateful Mouse run safely into the tall grass.\n\nA few weeks later, while walking through the dense forest, the Lion fell into a hunter's trap. Heavy rope nets fell from the tall branches and wrapped tightly around his body. The more the Lion struggled, the tighter the ropes pulled. The proud Lion was trapped! He roared with all his mighty strength, filling the whole valley with his desperate cries.\n\nFar away in her little hole, the tiny Mouse recognized the Lion's familiar roar. She ran as fast as her tiny legs could carry her toward the sound. When she arrived, she found the mighty Lion lying helpless on the ground, tied tightly by the thick ropes. "Do not worry, dear friend," whispered the brave Mouse. "I am here to help you."\n\nThe Mouse immediately set to work. With her sharp front teeth, she began to gnaw at the strong ropes. She chewed and bit through the heavy fibers, one knot after another. Within half an hour, the thick ropes broke apart. The great Lion stood up, free once again! He looked down at the tiny Mouse with sincere admiration. "You were right, little friend," said the Lion softly. "Even the smallest creature can help the mightiest beast."`,
