        id: "a2_pinocchio_c1",
        chapterNum: 1,
        title: "A Piece of Wood That Speaks",
        titleTr: "Konuşan Tahta Parçası",
        wordCount: 540,
        readTime: "4 dk",
        text: `Centuries ago, in a quaint Italian village, an elderly carpenter named Master Cherry found an unusual piece of wood in his workshop. It was not expensive timber, just a simple log meant for the fireplace. But as he raised his sharp axe to split it, a tiny, squeaky voice cried out: "Please do not strike me so hard!" Master Cherry froze in astonishment, dropping his tool to the floor.\n\nJust then, his neighbor Geppetto walked through the wooden door. Geppetto was a warm-hearted but poor old man with bright eyes and gray hair. "Good day, neighbor Cherry," said Geppetto cheerfully. "I have thought of a marvelous idea. I want to carve a wooden puppet that can dance, jump, and turn somersaults. I will travel across the world with him to earn my daily bread." Cherry, relieved to be rid of the mysterious log, gladly gifted the magical wood to his friend.\n\nGeppetto returned to his modest ground-floor cottage, where the only furniture was a wobbly table, an ancient chair, and a cold bed. He immediately began carving with great skill. "What name shall I give him?" he murmured thoughtfully. "I will call him Pinocchio. That name will bring him good fortune."\n\nFirst, Geppetto carved the puppet's curly hair, then the forehead, and finally the eyes. To his complete amazement, as soon as the eyes were finished, they began to move and stare boldly right into Geppetto's face! "Wooden eyes, why do you look at me like that?" asked the old woodcarver in disbelief. The puppet did not answer, but blinked mischievously.\n\nNext, Geppetto carved the nose. No sooner had he shaped it than the nose began to stretch and grow longer and longer. Geppetto cut it down with his knife, but the more he trimmed it, the longer that impertinent nose became! Soon it was stretching across the whole workbench. Finally, Geppetto carved the mouth. Before it was even finished, the mouth began to laugh mockingly at the old man.\n\n"Stop laughing at me!" cried Geppetto sternly. The mouth stopped laughing, but stuck out a long wooden tongue. At last, Geppetto carved the legs and feet. The moment Pinocchio felt his wooden feet touch the cold stone floor, he gave a wild leap, kicked Geppetto on the nose, and ran out through the open door into the bustling street! Geppetto ran after him, waving his arms and shouting: "Catch him! Catch him!" But Pinocchio ran like a scared rabbit, clattering his wooden shoes loudly on the village cobblestones.`,
